  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Transpositions shows early scholarly uptake and some pedagogical reach: it has been cited 3 times, appears in a YouTube discussion, and is held in library collections. The record also points to external support from the European Research Council and to open availability through the repository record.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Michael Schwab\u2019s <em>Transpositions</em> contributes to debates in artistic research by examining how research can generate new epistemic relationships that include aesthetic dimensions. The impact record suggests a work that is beginning to travel across adjacent fields rather than concentrating in one narrow lane. Its 3 citations, spread across 2020, 2022, and 2023, indicate an early but sustained scholarly signal.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing literature is small, but it is notably diverse in topic and venue. The book is taken up in articles appearing in communication, art, and music-related contexts, with citations linked to education, doctoral education, philosophical analysis, and artistic and creative research. That pattern fits the book\u2019s interdisciplinary framing and suggests it is being read as a conceptual resource for work on research-creation and related forms of inquiry.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also a modest teaching footprint. A YouTube video discusses the book, and library holdings place it in subject areas spanning theory of music and musicology as well as arts and philosophy. These signals do not prove curriculum adoption on their own, but they do show that the book is visible in learning and reference environments.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The metadata further records support from the European Research Council and an open-access repository instance for the book. Taken together, the evidence points to a scholarly monograph with a clear intellectual niche, a small but real citation trail, and signs of circulation in teaching and library settings. The record is still early, but it already shows the book moving within the communities most likely to build on its ideas.</p>",
